<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Semantic Feature Guidance</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our blockwise modeling enables efficient inference and segment-wise specialization, the temporal scope of each velocity block restricts its exposure to the clean data distribution. In particular, blocks operating in early timesteps primarily observe noisy samples, making it challenging to capture high-level semantic features.
Motivated by recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> demonstrating the effectiveness of semantic representations in generative performance, we propose a semantic conditioning technique, called <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Feature Guidance</span> (SemFeat), which enhances each velocity block by conditioning rich semantic context <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>a).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, we introduce a <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">shared</span> feature alignment network <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> which aims to extract robust semantic features from noisy intermediate states <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. To ensure these extracted features carry strong global semantic information, we train this alignment network by aligning its output features with embeddings from pretrained visual encoder <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as DINOv2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. Given an intermediate noisy input <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> at timestep <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the alignment network produces a semantic feature: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}=f_{\phi}(x_{t},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}=f_{\phi}(x_{t},c)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\times[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}:\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}\times[0,1]\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d_{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes conditioning information. This feature is trained to match the pretrained embedding <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{*}=\mathcal{E}(x_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{*}=\mathcal{E}(x_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the following feature alignment loss:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}(\phi,\psi):=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1},t}\left[d\left(h_{\psi}(f_{\phi}(x_{t},c)),h^{*}\right)\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}(\phi,\psi):=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1},t}\left[d\left(h_{\psi}(f_{\phi}(x_{t},c)),h^{*}\right)\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> is the learnable projection MLP layer to match the dimensionalities, and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a distance metric such as cosine similarity. Then, the velocity block <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> takes the current state <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, timestep <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, condiction <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, and semantic feature <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as inputs to estimate the conditional velocity field: <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{t}^{(m)}=v_{\theta}^{(m)}(x_{t},c,f_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{t}^{(m)}=v_{\theta}^{(m)}(x_{t},c,f_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. The blockwise flow matching loss with the semantic feature guidance becomes:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{BFM}}(\theta,\phi):=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1},t}\left[\|\hat{v}_{t}^{(m)}-v_{t}^{(m)}\|^{2}\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>BFM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{BFM}}(\theta,\phi):=\mathbb{E}_{x_{0},x_{1},t}\left[\|\hat{v}_{t}^{(m)}-v_{t}^{(m)}\|^{2}\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the overall training objective is a weighted sum of the blockwise flow matching and feature alignment losses:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{BFM}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>BFM</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>align</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{BFM}}+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{\text{align}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> controls the contribution of the feature alignment objective. Refer to Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Appendix B Analysis details ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for training pseudo code. This conditioning strategy allows each block to access semantically rich guidance, significantly improving generation quality. As demonstrated in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Blockwise Flow Matching (BFM) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, integrating SemFeat into BFM (third row in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Blockwise Flow Matching (BFM) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) achieves substantial FID gains compared to the vanilla BFM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparision with REPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.</span> Our SemFeat is inspired by recent works in representation-aligned diffusion, particularly REPA. However, there is a key difference in design: REPA aligns the internal hidden states of the velocity model directly with semantic features. In contrast, SemFeat introduces a dedicated alignment network <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> whose output is explicitly conditioned on the velocity blocks, thereby separating feature alignment from velocity prediction. We observe that this modular design leads to a more coherent and rich representation (Figure <a href="#S5.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Effect of temporal segments. ‣ 5.3 Analysis ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and improved generation quality (Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Training at higher resolution. ‣ 5.2 Generalization and Scalability ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.21167v1/figure/main_qual.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/181;" width="452" height="181" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Samples from BFM-XL<math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{SF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">SF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{SF}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on ImageNet <math id="S4.F4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>256</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with classifier-free guidance (<math id="S4.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w=4.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w=4.0</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Feature Residual Approximation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The feature alignment network <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> significantly improves generation quality by providing rich semantic guidance. However, evaluating the alignment network at every timestep during inference can undermine the efficiency gains by our BFM. To address this, we propose training a lightweight network that enables an efficient approximation of semantic features during inference (Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>b).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, after the initial training phase where we jointly optimize the feature alignment network <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and the velocity blocks <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we freeze the parameters of both sets of networks.
Then, in a second training stage, we introduce a smaller network <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted as Feature Residual Network (FRN), to approximate semantic features within the <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th temporal segment <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{m-1},t_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{m-1},t_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>. This is motivated by the observation in Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Training at higher resolution. ‣ 5.2 Generalization and Scalability ‣ 5 Experiment ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> that the discrepancy between start point feature <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t_{m-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t_{m-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and intermediate feature <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> increases as <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> increases within <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[t_{m-1},t_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[t_{m-1},t_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the start point <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t_{m-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t_{m-1}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we first compute its corresponding semantic feature using the alignment network: <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t_{m-1}}=f_{\phi}(x_{t_{m-1}},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t_{m-1}}=f_{\phi}(x_{t_{m-1}},c)</annotation></semantics></math>. For the subsequent timestep <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[t_{m-1},t_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[t_{m-1},t_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate its feature incrementally via a residual connection defined by: <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}_{t}=f_{t_{m-1}}+b_{m}(t)\cdot f_{\eta}(x_{t},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}_{t}=f_{t_{m-1}}+b_{m}(t)\cdot f_{\eta}(x_{t},c)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{m}(t)=\frac{t-t_{m-1}}{t_{m}-t_{m-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{m}(t)=\frac{t-t_{m-1}}{t_{m}-t_{m-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> is normalized offset from <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This allows the network <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> to scale the residual contribution based on how far the timestep <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is from the segment start point <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{m-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{m-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
FRN <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to match the output of the frozen alignment network <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> at intermediate timestep <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}=f_{\phi}(x_{t},c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}=f_{\phi}(x_{t},c)</annotation></semantics></math>, using the following feature regression loss:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{FRN}}(\eta):=\mathbb{E}\left[\,\|\hat{f}_{t}-f_{t}\|^{2}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>FRN</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{FRN}}(\eta):=\mathbb{E}\left[\,\|\hat{f}_{t}-f_{t}\|^{2}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">By leveraging this lightweight residual approximation, we drastically reduce inference cost, since the feature alignment network <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is only computed once per segment, and subsequent semantic features within each segment are computed efficiently by the FRN (see Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Method ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>c).
Empirically, our analysis demonstrates that this residual approximation (third row in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Blockwise Flow Matching (BFM) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) retains over 98% of the BFM-S<math id="S4.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}_{\text{SF}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>SF</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}_{\text{SF}}</annotation></semantics></math> fidelity while reducing inference complexity by 41% (5.01 to 2.96). Refer to Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix B Analysis details ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for pseudo code.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Inference</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At the beginning of the <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>-th segment, we compute the semantic feature <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t_{m-1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t_{m-1}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the feature alignment network <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>. For subsequent timesteps <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[t_{m-1},t_{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[t_{m-1},t_{m})</annotation></semantics></math>, we approximate the semantic feature either using FRN <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math> or the full alignment network <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the desired trade-off between speed and fidelity.
Then, the velocity block <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}^{(m)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}^{(m)}</annotation></semantics></math> predicts the velocity: <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{t}^{(m)}=v_{\theta}^{(m)}(x_{t},c,f_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{t}^{(m)}=v_{\theta}^{(m)}(x_{t},c,f_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The intermediate state is updated iteratively via a numerical solver using the predicted velocity.
This process repeats until reaching <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the final sample <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}\sim p_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}\sim p_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
A full pseudocode implementation of the inference process is provided in the Appendix (Algorithm <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ Discrepancy between 𝑓_𝑡_{𝑚-1} and 𝑓_𝑡 (Figure ). ‣ Appendix B Analysis details ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ Discrepancy between 𝑓_𝑡_{𝑚-1} and 𝑓_𝑡 (Figure ). ‣ Appendix B Analysis details ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiment</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We primarily compare our method with recent flow matching models built on diffusion transformer architectures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, demonstrating state-of-the-art performance in generative tasks. We follow the experimental setup established by SiT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> and REPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Most of the experiments are conducted on the ImageNet dataset at a resolution of 256 <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> 256, unless stated otherwise. We utilize an off-the-shelf pretrained VAE from Stable Diffusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which applies an 8× downsampling factor. We evaluate generation quality using several established metrics, including FID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>, sFID <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite>, Inception Score (IS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite>, Precision, and Recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>. Following recent architectural best practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, we adopt a DiT-style transformer backbone for both <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\eta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\eta}</annotation></semantics></math>. To integrate conditions to <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sum the semantic feature <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the timestep embeddings element-wise and inject it at the attention layers via AdaLN-Zero modulation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Our model configurations are based on -S and -XL architectures with a patch size of 2 and <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=6</annotation></semantics></math>. More implementation details are available in the Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Implementation details ‣ Blockwise Flow Matching: Improving Flow Matching Models For Efficient High-Quality Generation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">System-level comparison<span id="S5.T2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> on ImageNet <math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256\times 256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>256</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256\times 256</annotation></semantics></math> class-conditioned generation with classifier-free guidance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>. FLOPs are averaged across the solver steps and number of samples. <math id="S5.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math> indicate whether lower
or higher values are better, respectively. <sup id="S5.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S5.T2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">†</span></sup> use of guidance scheduling.</span></span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.7" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:142.8pt;vertical-align:-70.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-134.8pt,55.8pt) scale(0.561381289882483,0.561381289882483) ;">
